Subject: [PATCH] Stabilise drag v2: stop the peak tie from flipping, keep the
 rest of the stability fixes

Same fixes as the first attempt (hysteresis release, non-maximum
suppression, global nearest-neighbour tracking, ABS_X/ABS_Y following the
longest-held contact, one-pole low-pass with a fast-move bypass), plus the
change that the diagonal-jitter report actually called for.

The remaining defect was in candidate selection.  find_points() sorted its
3x3 maxima by peak strength only, so for the two (or four) nodes a finger sits
between, the winner could still flip from frame to frame: the centroid window
moved one cell diagonally, x and y jumped ~53 px at once, and the window the
compositor is dragging snapped sideways.  A horizontal drag stays inside one
sensor row, so the winner never changed and the symptom did not appear there.

Candidates are now a named struct at file scope and are ordered

    tracked contact first -> stronger peak -> scan order

so the peak that already belongs to a tracked finger always wins the tie, and
scan order makes every remaining tie deterministic instead of frame-dependent.
Because the sorted list drives non-maximum suppression, this also stops a
fresh neighbour peak from cancelling a contact the tracker is holding.

The first attempt also selected the ABS_X/ABS_Y source slot with an inverted
comparison: `oldest_age` started at INT32_MAX and was updated on
`slot.age < oldest_age`, so the variables said "held longest" while the code
picked the youngest contact -- the compositor moves the dragged window from
these two axes, so the drag followed whichever finger had landed last.  Fixed
to start at -1 and compare with `>`.

Threshold default, report rate, coordinate transform and pen decode unchanged.

